{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"CORRECTION TO MY EARLIER NOTE ON THIS TICKET (the cross-ticket note from d23e). I argued that this ticket's kill switch had a DURABILITY problem — that a runbook naming REBAR_LLM_MODEL as the recovery lever would rot when the variable was removed at the next major. THAT CONCERN IS WITHDRAWN.\n\nd23e has since adopted _permanent(...) rather than _scheduled(...), matching all six existing env-var deprecations in _deprecations.REGISTRY. _permanent sets remove_in=None with permanent=True: the variable warns and is NEVER REMOVED. So REBAR_LLM_MODEL keeps working indefinitely, this ticket's kill switch never rots, and there is no durability problem to solve.\n\nWHAT STANDS, reduced to its honest size: expressing the kill switch in class terms is now a COHERENCE preference — it would match the direction the epic establishes — and not a fix for anything. Keeping 'REBAR_LLM_MODEL back to anthropic:...' is entirely defensible, especially as the shortest possible operator action under incident pressure. The only thing worth doing either way is having the runbook note that the variable is a deprecated alias and name the class-based equivalent alongside, so a reader is not misled about which is the primary interface.\n\nNo action required on this ticket. I am recording the withdrawal rather than silently dropping it, because a stale argument left on a ticket is worse than no argument."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785586008628994001,"uuid":"06ecb759-b62d-429b-a982-20b9ba704eb9"}